sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2024-collective-10> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Single neural crest cells transposed to a different anterior-posterior axial domain change their gene expression to match their neighbours, whereas groups of transposed cells keep their original gene expression, which the authors interpret as collectives having greater positional memory (a perceptual field extending further into the past) than individual cells.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Grafted groups of neural crest cells keep their identity; single grafted cells do not";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "McMillen P, Levin M, “Collective intelligence: A unifying concept for integrating biology across scales and substrates”, Communications biology (2024), doi:10.1038/s42003-024-06037-4; section “Introduction › Neural crest intelligence is collective”. Scope: Vertebrate neural crest (chick, mouse) transplantation"
}
